REPORT AI FEASIBILITY MATRIX
Read-only analysis of a shared Horizon report workspace
Generated: 2026-10-04

Scope and privacy
-----------------
This analysis inspected report-workspace structure and sanitized template vocabulary only.
It did not read or reproduce the report narrative, client name, property address, financial
data, contact data, existing photos, or report findings. No report control was selected and
no report was saved, previewed, generated, published, sent, downloaded, or changed.

Observed report structure
-------------------------
The active workspace has:
- 165 interactive controls.
- 133 template/item controls.
- 4 editable text areas and 4 editable text inputs.
- photo-loading and gallery-related controls.
- required-item/completion checks.
- templates, database search, mobile-log, preview, work-order, and separate report-publishing
  workflows.
- standardized roof-related vocabulary: orientation, roof-covering/membrane/flashing
  materials, slope descriptors, and age choices.

This is important: the report is a structured inspection database with generated narrative,
not simply a word-processing document. The new system should retain structured observations,
evidence links, standardized choices, and validation rules.

Automation confidence tiers
---------------------------
Tier A: deterministic automation
No AI judgment is needed. Software rules should execute these consistently.

| Capability | Implementation | Human action |
|---|---|---|
| Photo upload retry/checksum | Mobile sync queue and storage checksum | None, except failed-sync recovery |
| Duplicate media detection | Perceptual hash + checksum | Review only if flagged |
| Required template item check | Rules engine against selected template | Resolve missing items |
| Required photo check | Rules engine by category/item | Capture/waive with reason |
| Mandatory report fields | Schema validation | Supply/confirm information |
| Report template rendering | Versioned HTML/PDF renderer | Review before publishing |
| Status/permission transitions | Server-side policy engine | Authorized action |
| Audit log/revision creation | Immutable event/revision service | None |
| Structured dropdown values | Configured controlled vocabulary | Choose/correct value |

Tier B: high-value AI assistance
AI can make a useful suggestion, but it must be visibly reviewable.

| Report area | AI can assist with | Why it can help | Required human review |
|---|---|---|---|
| Photo intake | Blur, darkness, duplicate, poor framing | Visual quality is measurable | Retake decision |
| Photo routing | Suggest exterior, roofing, electrical, plumbing, HVAC, interior, etc. | Common components are visually distinctive | Confirm category |
| Roof template | Suggest material/orientation/visible surface condition | Current template has structured choices | Confirm material and condition |
| Equipment labels | OCR serial/model/manufacturer text | Clear labels are machine-readable | Confirm OCR against photo |
| Captions | Draft factual caption from visible component | Reduces repetitive typing | Edit/approve wording |
| Voice notes | Transcribe and format | Inspector supplies the real context | Verify transcription |
| Report routing | Suggest report section/item from photo + note | Templates constrain output | Confirm item/location |
| Standard wording | Turn accepted structured choices into company style | Approved template language is controlled | Approve final observation |
| Completeness | Flag likely missing photos/items or contradictions | Combines rules and evidence inventory | Decide whether omission is valid |
| Summary draft | Summarize accepted observations | Reduces final writing time | Review for accuracy/priority |

Tier C: AI may flag a visible cue, but inspector judgment is mandatory

| Photo/evidence cue | Safe AI output | Inspector must determine |
|---|---|---|
| Stain/discoloration | "Possible staining visible" | Cause, active vs historic moisture, severity |
| Crack | "Crack/linear feature visible" | Structural significance, monitoring/repair need |
| Corrosion | "Corrosion visible at connection/surface" | Functional impact, safety, correction |
| Roof damage | "Possible missing/damaged material visible" | Leak risk, repair scope, roof condition |
| Electrical panel | "Possible opening/labeling/visible irregularity" | Electrical safety, code/repair recommendation |
| HVAC/water heater | Component/label/visible corrosion suggestion | Operation, age interpretation, service need |
| Plumbing | "Possible moisture/corrosion/leak evidence visible" | Active leak, source, damage extent |
| Stair/railing | "Handrail/guard condition may need review" | Code, safety compliance, repair details |
| Foundation | "Crack/staining feature visible" | Movement, structural effect, specialist referral |
| Appliance | Model/label/basic visible state | Functional testing, installation compliance |

Tier D: human-only professional decisions
----------------------------------------
AI must not make these decisions based on photos or notes:
- Whether an issue is a defect, safety hazard, code violation, or material failure.
- Whether a roof leaks, wiring is safe, a furnace operates correctly, or a crack is
  structural.
- Whether a component requires repair, replacement, monitoring, or specialist evaluation.
- Whether an inaccessible area is acceptable.
- Whether the inspection is complete.
- Final severity/classification and final recommendation.
- Report approval, signature, publication, or delivery.
- Insurance, legal, regulatory, financial, payment, refund, deletion, or export decisions.

Detailed photo-to-report decision flow
--------------------------------------
1. Inspector selects a category or takes an unclassified photo.
2. App performs Tier A quality and duplicate checks.
3. Vision/OCR service produces Tier B component/category and label suggestions.
4. If a possible visual cue is found, AI adds an editable marker and creates a Tier C
   "review candidate"; it does not create a final defect.
5. Inspector adds context: location, accessibility, functional test result, measurement,
   condition, limitation, and voice/text note.
6. AI produces a structured draft using only:
   - accepted template choices;
   - the inspector's note;
   - source media/evidence;
   - company-approved wording.
7. Inspector accepts, edits, rejects, or marks "needs verification."
8. Only accepted/edited items become report observations.
9. Tier A required-item validation runs before preview.
10. An authorized reviewer publishes an immutable report version.

Minimum data needed for trustworthy AI suggestions
--------------------------------------------------
Photo alone is insufficient for most inspection decisions. Capture these fields:
- Template category and component.
- Exact location/room/system.
- Inspector-selected visible condition.
- Accessibility/inspection limitation.
- Functional-test result where applicable.
- Measurement or equipment label where relevant.
- One or more evidence photos/markers.
- Inspector voice/text note.
- Company-approved report language and classification rules.

Recommended first AI release
----------------------------
Start with tasks likely to show useful quality:
1. Photo quality and duplicate detection.
2. Component/category suggestion.
3. OCR of clear equipment labels.
4. Photo caption draft.
5. Speech-to-text for inspector notes.
6. Structured report-item suggestion from photo + inspector note.
7. Missing-photo/required-item validation.
8. Draft summary of inspector-approved observations.

Do not launch automated issue detection as a product promise until measured against a
de-identified, inspector-reviewed test set for each category.

Quality gates before rollout
----------------------------
For each category (for example, roofing or electrical):
- Build a held-out inspector-reviewed evaluation set.
- Measure component recognition, photo-quality accuracy, OCR accuracy, evidence-marker
  overlap, false-positive rate, missed-cue rate, and inspector acceptance/edit/rejection rate.
- Set category-specific release thresholds with inspectors; do not use one global "accuracy"
  number.
- Launch in shadow mode: show suggestions to inspectors but do not create report items.
- Pilot with a small group, review errors weekly, and keep full manual entry available.
- Re-evaluate whenever the model, prompt, template, or classification rules change.
